---
import Landing from "../layouts/Landing.astro";
import Playground from "../components/Playground.astro";
import HeroShear from "../components/HeroShear.astro";

const demo = [
  "// The validator you already use.",
  "const Person = z.object({",
  "  name: z.string(),",
  "  age: z.int().nonnegative(),",
  '  sex: z.enum(["M", "F", "X"]),',
  "});",
  "",
  "// It is also the wire format.",
  "const bytes = encode(Person, person);",
  "// → 8 canonical bytes, validated before encoding",
  "",
  "const back = decode(Person, bytes);",
  '// → { name: string; age: number; sex: "M" | "F" | "X" }',
];

const payloads = [
  { label: "shorn", note: "", bytes: 8, self: true },
  { label: "Avro", note: "own schema", bytes: 8, self: false },
  { label: "SchemaPack", note: "own builder", bytes: 9, self: false },
  { label: "Protobuf", note: ".proto + codegen", bytes: 11, self: false },
  { label: "msgpackr plain", note: "field names", bytes: 25, self: false },
  { label: "JSON", note: "field names + text", bytes: 35, self: false },
];

---

<Landing
  title="shorn: compact binary serialization for Zod, Valibot, and ArkType"
  description="Binary serialization for the Zod, Valibot, or ArkType schema you already have. Install it and pass your schema to encode and decode. No schema file, no code generation, no second copy of your types."
  headline="Your validator is the wire format."
  size="md"
  cta="Read the docs"
  code={demo}
>
  Already have a Zod, Valibot, or ArkType schema? Then you already have a binary
  format. Install shorn and pass that schema to <code>encode</code> and
  <code>decode</code>. There is no schema file to write, no code to generate, and
  nothing to migrate.

  {/* The argument itself, not a table about it: the same record as text and as bytes,
      and the shearing of one into the other. */}
  <HeroShear slot="figure" />

  <section slot="after" class="plain drop-in" aria-labelledby="drop-in">
    <h2 id="drop-in">Install it. Keep your schema.</h2>
    <p>
      shorn reads the wire layout from your schema. Zod, Valibot, or ArkType still
      owns validation, transformations, and error messages, exactly as before.
    </p>
    <dl class="setup">
      <div class="self">
        <dt>shorn</dt>
        <dd>
          <strong>Nothing to set up.</strong> Call
          <code>encode(schema, value)</code> and <code>decode(schema, bytes)</code>
          with the validator you already have.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Avro</dt>
        <dd>
          Write and maintain a second schema in Avro's own format, then build an Avro
          codec from it.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>SchemaPack</dt>
        <dd>Describe the same shape a second time with SchemaPack's builder.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Protobuf</dt>
        <dd>
          Write a <code>.proto</code> file, then either generate code from it or set up
          runtime reflection.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    {/* The size comparison belongs with the setup cost, not in the hero: it is the same
        list of formats, answering the second question you ask about each of them. */}
    <figure class="sizes">
      <table>
        <caption>That same Person record, encoded by each of them.</caption>
        <tbody>
          {
            payloads.map((row) => (
              <tr class:list={{ self: row.self }}>
                <th scope="row">
                  {row.label}{row.note && <small> ({row.note})</small>}
                </th>
                <td class="count">{row.bytes}</td>
                <td class="bar" aria-hidden="true">
                  <span>
                    {Array.from({ length: row.bytes }, () => (
                      <i />
                    ))}
                  </span>
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <figcaption>
        shorn matches Avro's eight bytes without asking you to write an Avro schema.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <Playground slot="after" />

  <section slot="after" class="plain" aria-labelledby="fit">
    <h2 id="fit">Built for shared TypeScript schemas</h2>
    <div class="fit">
      <p class="in">
        Use it where both ends are TypeScript and share the schema: caches, RPC, worker
        messages, job queues, and compact application storage.
      </p>
      <p class="out">
        Use something else when a reader is in another language, when a payload has to
        describe itself, or when the two ends need to change schemas independently.
        <code>fingerprinted()</code> stamps the wire shape into the bytes, so a payload
        written by an older shape is rejected instead of decoded into the wrong value.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section slot="after" class="plain" aria-labelledby="wire-safety">
    <h2 id="wire-safety">Safe to put on the wire</h2>
    <div class="proofs">
      <article>
        <h3>Canonical bytes</h3>
        <p>
          The same value always encodes to exactly the same bytes, so a payload can serve
          as a cache key or a content hash.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Defensive decoding</h3>
        <p>
          Decoding bytes you did not write is where binary formats get exploited. The
          decoder checks every length against the input before it allocates, and rejects
          invalid UTF-8 and leftover bytes.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section slot="after" class="plain" aria-labelledby="size">
    <h2 id="size">Smallest or tied in every raw fixture</h2>
    <p>
      Small payloads normally cost CPU, because a compressor has to produce them. These
      come from the schema instead, in the same pass that encodes the value.
    </p>

    {/* "100-event payload" meant nothing without the shape behind it. This is the
        literal fixture from bench/fixtures.mjs, so keep the two in step. */}
    <pre class="fixture"><code>{`Person  { age, name, sex }
Event   { active, actor: Person, id,
          metrics: { cpu, memory }, tags[], timestamp }`}</code></pre>
    <p class="quiet fixture-note">
      Four fixtures: a Person, the same Person with non-ASCII text, one Event, and a
      batch of 100 Events in an array.
    </p>

    <dl class="setup">
      <div class="self">
        <dt>4.2 MB</dt>
        <dd>
          <strong>for 100,000 events</strong> that take 16 MB as JSON, before any
          compressor runs. Gzipping those shorn bytes afterwards costs a further 48 ms
          of CPU.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>up to 6.2×</dt>
        <dd>faster than JSON to encode, and up to 13.7× faster to decode.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>up to 77%</dt>
        <dd>smaller than JSON before compression.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p class="quiet">
      <a
        href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "")}/performance/size/`}
        >Read the results, caveats, and reproduction steps</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
</Landing>

<style>
  .plain {
    padding: clamp(48px, 8vh, 80px) 0 clamp(36px, 5vh, 56px);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--shorn-rule-soft);
  }

  .plain h2 {
    margin: 0 0 10px;
    font-size: 21px;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  .plain h3 {
    margin: 0 0 6px;
    font-size: 16px;
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .plain p {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 68ch;
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--shorn-ink-2);
  }

  .proofs {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 240px), 1fr));
    gap: 28px;
    max-width: 1040px;
    margin-top: 22px;
  }

  .setup {
    margin: 24px 0 0;
    max-width: 900px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--shorn-rule-soft);
  }

  .setup > div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(120px, 170px) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 24px;
    padding: 14px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--shorn-rule-soft);
  }

  .setup dt,
  .setup dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .setup dt {
    font-family: var(--mono, ui-monospace), monospace;
    color: var(--shorn-ink-3);
  }

  .setup dd {
    color: var(--shorn-ink-2);
  }

  .setup strong {
    color: var(--shorn-ink);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .setup .self dt {
    color: var(--shorn-ink);
  }

  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .setup > div {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 3px;
    }
  }

  /* The boundary drawn as a boundary: one rule down the side, lit where shorn belongs
     and dim where it does not. The sentences name their own side, so no headings. */
  .fit {
    display: grid;
    gap: 18px;
    max-width: 68ch;
    margin-top: 20px;
  }

  .fit p {
    padding-left: 18px;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--shorn-rule);
  }

  .fit .in {
    color: var(--shorn-ink);
    border-left-color: var(--shorn-ink);
  }

  .fit .out {
    color: var(--shorn-ink-3);
  }

  .plain ul {
    margin: 14px 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.1em;
    max-width: 68ch;
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--shorn-ink-2);
  }

  .plain li::marker {
    color: var(--shorn-ink-3);
  }

  .plain .quiet {
    margin-top: 16px;
    font-size: 14.5px;
    color: var(--shorn-ink-3);
  }

  .plain code {
    font-family: var(--mono, ui-monospace), monospace;
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }

  .plain a {
    color: inherit;
  }

  /* The fixture shapes, so "100-event payload" means something. A plain block, not the
     hero's code treatment: it is a shape sketch, not runnable source. */
  .fixture {
    margin: 18px 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-family: var(--mono, ui-monospace), monospace;
    font-size: 13.5px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--shorn-ink-2);
  }

  .fixture-note {
    margin-top: 10px;
  }


  /* ---------- the size table, now in the setup section ---------- */
  .sizes {
    margin: 30px 0 0;
    max-width: 60ch;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .sizes table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 14px;
  }

  .sizes caption {
    margin-bottom: 14px;
    text-align: left;
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--shorn-ink-3);
  }

  .sizes th,
  .sizes td {
    /* 4px each side against a 14px cell is a 22px row, the rhythm the old
       gap-based version had, minus the rows that silently grew to two lines. */
    padding: 4px 0;
    font-family: var(--mono, ui-monospace), monospace;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--shorn-ink-3);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* Right-aligned, the way a horizontal bar chart always sets its category labels.
     Left-aligned, the column is as wide as "msgpackr plain (field names)" and every
     short row opens a void between its name and its count: "shorn" at one edge and
     "8" stranded at the other. Flush right, name and count sit together and the bars
     still share a left edge. */
  .sizes th {
    padding-right: 20px;
    text-align: right;
  }

  .sizes th small {
    font: inherit;
    color: inherit;
  }

  /* The count leads the bars rather than trailing them, so the bars share a left
     edge and stay directly comparable, and the numbers stay a clean column. */
  .sizes .count {
    padding-right: 16px;
    text-align: right;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .sizes .bar span {
    display: flex;
    gap: 2px;
  }

  .sizes .bar i {
    width: clamp(4px, 0.45vw, 6px);
    height: 14px;
    border-radius: 2px;
    background: var(--shorn-mark-dim);
  }

  .sizes tr.self th,
  .sizes tr.self .count {
    color: var(--shorn-ink);
  }

  .sizes tr.self .bar i {
    background: var(--shorn-ink);
  }

  figcaption {
    margin-top: 18px;
    max-width: 62ch;
    font-size: 14.5px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--shorn-ink-3);
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .sizes th small {
      display: none;
    }

    .sizes .bar span {
      gap: 1px;
    }
  }
</style>
